        "deliverables": [
          {
            "id": "deliverable:obj_1790305036274_96t4b4:objective/deliverable",
            "title": "Objective deliverable",
            "type": "document",
            "version": 4,
            "status": "current",
            "summary": "Added a concise proposed seven-day launch-week cadence and channel-specific publishing sequence, preserving existing supported launch context, positioning, recommendations, provenance, limitations, and review-only status. Not published.",
            "content": "# Somebody launch context and channel recommendations\n\n## Launch situation\nThe company brief reports weak signup conversion and says many visitors do not quickly recognize that Somebody is for founders of very small companies or understand how it differs from other AI tools. The page currently leans on broad “AI manager” and “automate your workflows” language; founder wording for the underlying problem has not been established. This is a reported diagnosis, not a quantified baseline or validated cause. (Company records: `launch/context`; product context: `company/profile`.)\n\n## Positioning direction\nSomebody serves founders running one-person companies and lean teams. It helps organize work, bring in outside help when needed, and stay with an objective until there is a usable result, without adding another tool to manage. Keep the promise practical: do not imply Somebody replaces the founder or guarantees a business result. (Company record: `company/profile`.)\n\n**Proposed message for review:** “For founders running a one-person company or a lean team: keep important work moving without adding another tool to manage. Somebody helps organize the work, bring in outside help when needed, and stay with an objective through to a usable result.” This is a positioning hypothesis, not founder-validated customer language or evidence of a conversion lift.\n\n## Channel recommendations\nThe comparative input is a **synthetic Testnet social-intelligence dataset** generated by a controlled Somebody test provider. No live TikTok, Instagram, Facebook, or X data was queried; it is not a live firehose, causal attribution, or conversion measurement. Use its patterns only as hypotheses for a measured creative pilot, not as proof of reach or performance. (Acquired evidence: `ev_result_a292822a3d167c8af9d0dfc3`.)\n\nThe benchmark suggests that concrete founder pain in the first two seconds of short-form vertical video retains attention better than brand introductions; before/after desk-day montages under 25 seconds for TikTok and Reels; tool-versus-accountable-system carousels; myth-busting text overlays on founder photos for Instagram/Facebook; and numbered pain-to-fix threads on X. It also favors concrete workflow examples over abstract AI claims. These are synthetic comparative claims, not live platform analytics.\n\n- **TikTok:** Test a sub-25-second before/after desk-day montage. Open with a specific founder workload situation, then show a practical way Somebody helps organize work and keep an objective moving. Message: “When you’re carrying every part of the business, keep the next important thing moving—without another tool to manage.”\n- **Instagram:** Adapt the video for Reels; test a feed carousel contrasting another tool to manage with a system helping carry an objective toward a usable result. An alternate static concept uses a myth-busting overlay. Keep product claims practical and non-guaranteed.\n- **Facebook:** Adapt the static founder-photo concept, pairing a myth-busting overlay with one specific workflow example and a question inviting founders to share what is weighing on them this week.\n- **X:** Test a numbered founder pain → practical fix thread using a concrete workflow example; invite replies about work that is hard to keep moving. Avoid generic automation claims.\n\nThe benchmark excerpt ends after claiming a “what are you drowning in this week?” question outperforms; that incomplete claim is not independently verified or usable as a measured result. Treat questions only as creative hypotheses. The proposed channel sequence is a test recommendation based on format fit, not a ranking of audience reach.\n\n## Launch-week cadence (proposed, review-only)\n- **Day 1:** TikTok and Instagram Reels: paired short-form concepts with the same workflow and audience-explicit message.\n- **Day 2:** X: numbered pain-to-fix thread; invite replies.\n- **Day 3:** Instagram feed: test the tool-versus-accountable-system carousel.\n- **Day 4:** Facebook: adapt the static myth-busting concept with a concrete workflow and open question.\n- **Day 5:** TikTok and Reels: post a distinct hook/workflow variation if capacity allows; otherwise review early observations before making another version.\n- **Day 6:** X: share a follow-up workflow example or respond to a relevant audience question without promising outcomes.\n- **Day 7:** Review what was posted, platform-native results, and qualitative replies. Do not declare a winner from impressions or engagement alone; compare qualified visits and signups only if tracking and a consistent landing page are in place.\n\nThis is a suggested cadence, not an evidence-based optimal frequency. Confirm capacity and current platform guidance before execution. This plan does not publish content.\n\n## Public platform context and limitations\nInstagram’s official *Instagram Ranking Explained* (May 31, 2023) says Instagram has no single algorithm and that Feed, Stories, Explore, Reels, Search, and other surfaces use their own processes. It does not establish a specific reach tactic, current performance trend, or likely result for Somebody. Source: Instagram, https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained (page observed in prior research; retrieved text was truncated).\n\nAvailable evidence does not establish which platform best reaches Somebody’s audience, validate platform-specific organic tactics, or show that any proposed message will improve conversion. The launch brief supplies no conversion metric or causal proof. Unknowns include founder-validated problem language, conversion baseline and experiment results, comparative reach for the target audience, and whether synthetic benchmark patterns generalize.\n\n## Review and next steps\nKeep this plan internal and unpublished. Validate problem language with founders. Record the signup-conversion baseline before testing; compare formats and audience-explicit messages against a consistent audience-explicit landing page, tracking qualified visits and signups. Treat results as evidence to review, not a guarantee, and reassess channel priorities using live, audience-relevant evidence.\n\n## Evidence\nCompany records: `company/profile` and `launch/context` (application-observed). Comparative input: synthetic controlled Testnet dataset, `ev_result_a292822a3d167c8af9d0dfc3`; no live platform query, causal attribution, or conversion measurement. Public context: Instagram’s official ranking explanation linked above.",
            "updatedAt": 1790305723123
          }
        ],
