        "deliverables": [
          {
            "id": "deliverable:obj_1790305036274_96t4b4:objective/deliverable",
            "title": "Objective deliverable",
            "type": "document",
            "version": 5,
            "status": "current",
            "summary": "Revised the founder-ready launch plan to address review feedback: the cadence is now presented as the actionable launch-week schedule rather than review-only, while preserving evidence provenance, material limitations, positioning, and the…",
            "content": "# Somebody launch-week social media plan\n\n## Launch context and message\nThe company brief reports weak signup conversion and says many visitors do not quickly recognize that Somebody is for founders of very small companies or understand how it differs from other AI tools. The page currently uses broad “AI manager” and “automate your workflows” language. Founder wording for the underlying problem has not been established; this is a reported diagnosis, not a quantified baseline or validated cause. (Company records: `launch/context`; `company/profile`.)\n\nSomebody serves founders running one-person companies and lean teams. It helps organize work, bring in outside help when needed, and stay with an objective until there is a usable result, without adding another tool to manage. Keep the promise practical: do not imply Somebody replaces the founder or guarantees a business result. (Company record: `company/profile`.)\n\n**Core message:** “For founders running a one-person company or a lean team: keep important work moving without adding another tool to manage. Somebody helps organize the work, bring in outside help when needed, and stay with an objective through to a usable result.” This is a positioning hypothesis, not founder-validated customer language or evidence of a conversion lift.\n\n## Channel approach\nThe comparative input is a **synthetic Testnet social-intelligence dataset** generated by a controlled Somebody test provider. No live TikTok, Instagram, Facebook, or X data was queried. It is not live platform analytics, a live firehose, causal attribution, or conversion measurement. The patterns below are creative hypotheses, not proof of reach or performance. (Acquired evidence: `ev_result_a292822a3d167c8af9d0dfc3`.)\n\nThe dataset suggests using concrete founder pain early in short-form video, before/after desk-day montages under 25 seconds for TikTok and Reels, tool-versus-accountable-system carousels, myth-busting text overlays on founder photos for Instagram/Facebook, and numbered pain-to-fix threads on X. It favors concrete workflow examples over abstract AI claims. These are synthetic comparative claims, not live platform analytics.\n\n- **TikTok:** Publish a sub-25-second before/after desk-day montage. Open with a specific founder workload situation, then show a practical way Somebody helps organize work and keep an objective moving. Use the core message; avoid implying guaranteed outcomes.\n- **Instagram:** Use a Reel adapted from the short-form video and a feed carousel contrasting another tool to manage with a system helping carry an objective toward a usable result. A static myth-busting founder-photo concept is an alternate format.\n- **Facebook:** Use a static founder-photo myth-busting concept with one specific workflow example and an open question about what is weighing on founders this week.\n- **X:** Publish a numbered founder pain → practical fix thread using a concrete workflow example; invite replies about work that is hard to keep moving. Avoid generic automation claims.\n\nThe benchmark excerpt ends after an incomplete claim that a particular question outperforms; it is not independently verified or usable as a measured result. Use questions as creative prompts only. This channel mix is a format-fit recommendation, not a ranking of audience reach.\n\n## Launch-week cadence\n- **Day 1:** TikTok and Instagram Reels: paired short-form concepts with the same workflow and audience-explicit message.\n- **Day 2:** X: numbered pain-to-fix thread; invite replies.\n- **Day 3:** Instagram feed: tool-versus-accountable-system carousel.\n- **Day 4:** Facebook: static myth-busting concept with a concrete workflow and open question.\n- **Day 5:** TikTok and Reels: distinct hook/workflow variation. If production capacity does not support it, use the day to review initial observations and prepare the next variation.\n- **Day 6:** X: follow-up workflow example or response to a relevant audience question, without promising outcomes.\n- **Day 7:** Review published content, platform-native results, and qualitative replies. Do not declare a winner from impressions or engagement alone. Compare qualified visits and signups only if tracking and a consistent landing page are in place.\n\nThis cadence is a practical starting schedule, not an evidence-based optimal frequency. Confirm team capacity and current platform guidance before execution.\n\n## Measurement, evidence limits, and next steps\nInstagram’s official *Instagram Ranking Explained* (May 31, 2023) says Instagram has no single algorithm and that Feed, Stories, Explore, Reels, Search, and other surfaces use their own processes. It does not establish a specific reach tactic, current performance trend, or likely result for Somebody. Source: Instagram, https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained (prior observation; retrieved text was truncated).\n\nAvailable evidence does not establish which platform best reaches Somebody’s audience, validate platform-specific organic tactics, or show that any proposed message will improve conversion. The launch brief supplies no conversion metric or causal proof. Unknowns include founder-validated problem language, conversion baseline and experiment results, comparative reach for the target audience, and whether synthetic benchmark patterns generalize. Before evaluating performance, record a signup-conversion baseline and use a consistent audience-explicit landing page; track qualified visits, signups, and qualitative feedback. Reassess channel priorities using live, audience-relevant evidence.\n\n## Evidence\nCompany records: `company/profile` and `launch/context` (application-observed). Comparative input: synthetic controlled Testnet dataset, `ev_result_a292822a3d167c8af9d0dfc3`; no live platform query, causal attribution, or conversion measurement. Public context: Instagram’s official ranking explanation linked above.",
            "updatedAt": 1790305764617
          }
        ],
